<h2>The Complete Guide to the 12 Seater Maharaja Urbania in Bangalore</h2>
<p>The 12 seater Maharaja Urbania is a different kind of rental from a standard group van. It is built for travellers who would rather have wide, business-class seats and a calm cabin than the maximum number of seats. This guide explains what the vehicle is, who it suits, how to plan an executive, family or celebration trip around it, what it costs with worked examples, and how it compares with the alternatives. All rates are the live Yogi Tours &amp; Travels tariff: ₹38 per km, a 300 km daily minimum and ₹700 per day driver Bata, with tolls, parking, permits and state taxes billed at actuals.</p>

<h2>Part 1. What Makes the Maharaja Urbania Different</h2>

<h3>The platform</h3>
<p>The Maharaja Urbania is built on the Force Urbania platform. The Urbania uses a monocoque body, which means the body and chassis are one structure, and it rides closer to a large car than to a traditional commercial van. The cabin is insulated for low noise, and the roof is high enough that most adults can stand and walk through the cabin. For a rental customer, these details translate into a calmer, steadier and quieter journey, especially over six or seven hours on hill roads.</p>

<h3>The Maharaja configuration</h3>
<p>The word "Maharaja" refers to the seating. Where the 17 seater Urbania maximises capacity, the Maharaja edition trades seats for space. It carries 12 passengers in a 2+1 and 1+1 layout, using flight business-class leather captain recliners trimmed in quilted tan and ivory leather. Each seat has an extendable calf rest for proper leg support, and the layout leaves a wide aisle. The cabin has dual blue neon ceiling light rails, a mounted Sony Bravia Smart TV, on-board cold storage for drinks, USB ports at every seat, wide tinted panoramic windows with privacy curtains, and a dedicated rear luggage boot sized for about 12 bags.</p>
<p>The result is a cabin that feels closer to a private lounge than to a bus. Passengers sit in individual leather chairs, recline when they want to rest, and have their own power and lighting. That is why executives and families who value comfort pick it, even though it costs more per kilometre than a Tempo Traveller.</p>

<h3>The two layouts, 2+1 and 1+1</h3>
<p>In a 2+1 row, two seats sit on one side of the aisle and one on the other. In a 1+1 row, there is one seat on each side. The 1+1 rows give a passenger a seat with an aisle on both sides, which is the most private seating in the vehicle. When you enquire, tell us who will travel. We suggest the most comfortable seats for the oldest or most senior passengers and for anyone who needs extra room.</p>

