<h2>Part 7. Comfort, Health and Safety</h2>

<h3>For senior travellers</h3>
<p>The recliners and calf rests let senior travellers rest properly. Seat them beside the door and in a 1+1 seat with aisle access. Plan stops every two hours and carry medicines in the cabin. Ask the driver to avoid sudden braking and to take ghat roads slowly.</p>

<h3>For motion sickness</h3>
<p>Pick a front seat, keep the AC louver pointed at the face, avoid reading on a phone and eat lightly before the ghat sections. A reclined seat that supports the head and neck often helps, and the Urbania's smoother ride helps further.</p>

<h3>For long drives</h3>
<p>Stay hydrated, stretch at stops and rotate seats. Share the driving schedule with the group so people know when the next stop is. A rested driver is the best safety feature of any trip. We plan driving within the 6:00 AM to 10:00 PM duty window wherever possible.</p>

<h3>For a day of meetings</h3>
<p>If you use the vehicle as a mobile office, agree a quiet cabin. The Smart TV can be turned off, and the ride stays steady enough for reading or light work. Bring a power bank as a backup, even though each seat has a USB port.</p>

<h2>Part 7B. The Executive Hospitality Playbook</h2>
<p>When the vehicle is part of a business relationship, small details carry weight. This section is a practical checklist for hosting guests in a Maharaja Urbania, whether you are a company arranging a client visit or an individual hosting an important family guest.</p>

<h3>Before the guest arrives</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Confirm the names and the count.</strong> Send us the guest list so the driver can greet each person correctly. A driver who knows a guest's name creates a better first moment than one holding a blank sign.</li>
<li><strong>Share the arrival details.</strong> The flight number, the scheduled arrival time and the terminal let the driver track delays. For train arrivals, share the train number and the platform if you have it.</li>
<li><strong>Agree the meeting point.</strong> Choose a clear place inside the arrivals area and tell both the guest and the driver. Keep a mobile number on both sides.</li>
<li><strong>Plan the first stop.</strong> Is the guest going straight to a meeting, to a hotel or to lunch? Give the driver the full address and a contact at the destination.</li>
<li><strong>Decide on refreshments.</strong> The on-board cold storage can hold bottled water and cold drinks. Tell us what you would like available so it is ready, and agree who pays for any items you ask the driver to buy.</li>
</ul>

<h3>During the journey</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Let the guest set the tone.</strong> Some guests want to talk, others want to rest after a long flight. The quiet cabin supports both.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the cabin temperature comfortable.</strong> Ask the guest, since people differ, and use the individual AC louvers.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the schedule visible.</strong> The coordinator should know the timing of the next meeting and tell the driver about any changes in time.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the driver informed.</strong> A chauffeur who knows the plan can warn you about traffic and suggest alternatives.</li>
</ul>

<h3>At the end of the trip</h3>
<ul>
<li>Check the cabin for forgotten items, such as chargers, jackets and documents.</li>
<li>Settle tolls and parking at the end of the day, and keep receipts for the company's records.</li>
<li>If the company needs a GST invoice, confirm the details before the trip so the invoice can be raised correctly.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Part 7C. Choosing Between the Maharaja Urbania and Other Vehicles</h2>
<p>A decision tree helps. Ask these questions in order.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>How many people are travelling?</strong> Up to 7 can go in an <a href="/fleet/car/innova-crysta">Innova Crysta</a> at ₹19 per km. Up to 9, consider the <a href="/fleet/tempo-traveller/9-seater-tempo-traveller">9 seater Tempo Traveller</a> at ₹28 per km. Up to 12, the Maharaja Urbania or the Maharaja Tempo Traveller. Up to 17, the 17 seater Urbania or Tempo Traveller. Up to 21, the mini bus.</li>
<li><strong>Who is travelling?</strong> VIP guests, executives and elderly relatives favour the Urbania. A group of colleagues on a budget favours the Tempo Traveller.</li>
<li><strong>How long is the journey?</strong> Over five hours, comfort matters more. Under two hours, it matters less.</li>
<li><strong>What is the budget?</strong> The Maharaja Urbania costs about ₹10,000 more than the Maharaja Tempo Traveller on a 600 km two-day trip. Decide whether the premium fits.</li>
<li><strong>How much luggage?</strong> The Maharaja Urbania carries about 12 bags. If you carry more, add a luggage vehicle or choose the 17 seater Urbania, which carries about 17.</li>
</ol>

